description:
This is a feature suggestion, although it may be redundant, as I am new to googlesheets4/API stuff.

I am looking to deploy a questionnaire using RShiny/gs4, and have an app working on my local computer using the non-interactive auth approach. However, I would like to also include a `while() { try() }` statement so that any API call fails are caught, but in this case the sheet already exists, so I cannot monitor for the creation of a sheet object using `is.null()` in the while clause. For example, in the below code example from my Shiny app, I need to monitor something else indicating success in the `while()` function to stop just the writing of 6 identical rows, or 6 pointless overwrites.

Looking at the code of `sheet_write()` or `sheet_append()` I was wondering whether it would be possible for the functions to optionally return some token of success (e.g. from `gargle::response_process()` so that the success of the call (or otherwise) can also be monitored within the `while()` function.

```r
server <- function(input, output, session) {
observeEvent(input$submit, {
    
    if(!imgID() %in% so_far$rand_id){ ## if new submission, just append answer
      
      x <- ids[ids$rand_id == imgID(), ]
      x$q1<- input$q1
      x$q2<- input$q2

      attempt <- 1
      while( attempt <= 6 ) { ## monitor something else to indicate success/failure here?
        attempt <- attempt + 1
        try(
          sheet_append(sheet_id, x, sheet = "Sheet1")
        )
      } 

    } else {
      # else just overwrite the specific updated row, but then rewrite all rows
      so_far[so_far$rand_id == imgID(),
           c('q1', 'q2')] <- list(input$q1,
                                           input$q2)
      
      attempt <- 1
      while( attempt <= 6 ) { ## also monitor something else to indicate success/failure here
        attempt <- attempt + 1
        try(
          sheet_write(so_far, ss = sheet_id, sheet = "Sheet1")
        )
      } 
}
```
Apologies if I am trying to do something stupid, or if this is dealt with elsewhere.
